  "summary": "The film-window exclusion and unchanged source-frame schedule preserve their properties. The doubled cadence-plus-uncertainty bound does not prove that a capture gap is native: it admits intervals with normal animation callbacks throughout a missing segment, and grows with capture slowness.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "blocking",
      "property": "Unexplained capture gaps stay inconclusive",
      "problem": "The bound applies twice the sum of median capture cadence and marker uncertainty to each endpoint and duration. Median capture cadence is not an upper bound on delivery uncertainty, and the factor of two permits missing spans beyond one source frame plus measured uncertainty.",
      "proof": "The first adversarial case changes from unexplained in the prior gate to native-animation-stall after the correction although raw callbacks continue for the first 60 ms of the 212 ms capture gap. The second classifies a 500 ms capture gap as native using only a 280 ms native interval.",
      "recommendation": "Keep questionable intervals inconclusive. Match interval endpoints using a bounded, independently justified time relationship; do not allow the recorder cadence itself to grow the exoneration tolerance, and include callback receipt evidence when attributing a stall."
    }
  ],
